# Build-time dependencies must survive a production install.
#
# Render sets NODE_ENV=production, and npm derives `omit=dev` from it — so a
# plain `npm install`/`npm ci` drops every devDependency. That breaks the build
# in a way that is genuinely hard to read:
#
#   - `typescript` SURVIVES, because @prisma/client (a production dependency)
#     lists it as an optional peer, so npm marks it `devOptional`. `tsc` runs.
#   - every `@types/*` package is plain `dev`, so none of them install.
#
# The result is a compiler that works perfectly and cannot find a single type
# declaration: ~60 errors reading "Could not find a declaration file for module
# 'express'", "Cannot find namespace 'PDFKit'", "Namespace 'global.Express' has
# no exported member 'Multer'". Nothing in that output points at the installer.
#
# `include` beats `omit` regardless of order or origin (npm documents this), and
# a project .npmrc outranks the NODE_ENV-derived default. So this holds even if
# the host's build command is a bare `npm install` — which is the point: the
# flag in render.yaml's buildCommand is lost the moment the service is
# configured by hand in the dashboard instead of from the blueprint.
#
# Trade-off, stated plainly: production installs now carry typescript and the
# @types packages (~50 MB of disk, zero runtime cost — nothing in dist/ imports
# them). That is the price of a build that cannot silently lose its types.
include=dev
